.TH QDOSCTL 1 "2026-10-10" "qdosctl 0.3.0" "QDOS Manual"
.SH NAME
qdosctl \- talk to a QDOS calculator over its USB serial console
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qdosctl
.RI [ OPTION ]...\&
.B upload
.IR PATH ...
.br
.B qdosctl
.RI [ OPTION ]...\&
.BR list " | " ping " | " console " | " log
.br
.B qdosctl
.RI [ OPTION ]...\&
.B remove
.IR NAME ...
.br
.B qdosctl
.RI [ OPTION ]...\&
.B push
.IR NUMBER ...
.br
.B qdosctl
.RI [ OPTION ]...\&
.BR run " | " type
.I TEXT
.br
.B qdosctl
.RI [ OPTION ]...\&
.B key
.IR NAME ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qdosctl
puts programs on a QDOS calculator, takes them off again, types at it and
shows what it logs, all over the USB cable it is plugged in by.
.PP
The calculator's USB port is a serial console: what is typed at it arrives as
key presses, and what it logs comes back. Files travel the same way, framed so
they are never taken for typing. They land on the
.IR inbox ,
the store for programs that come from a computer, and the calculator reads the
inbox again as soon as a file has arrived: a new app is in
.B APPS
at once, with no restart.
.PP
Only one program may have the port open at a time. Close a monitor or a
terminal on it before running
.BR qdosctl ,
and
.B qdosctl
before flashing.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI upload " PATH" ...
Put programs and apps on the inbox. A file is put there under its own name; a
folder is an app, and every file in it is put in a folder of that name, so
.I ../qdos\-doom/doom
becomes
.IR doom/main.qd ,
.I doom/doom1.wad
and so on. Each file is checked against its CRC\-32 when it has arrived, and
only then put in place, so the calculator never reads half of one. A file of
the same name is replaced. Names are letters, digits,
.BR . ", " _ " and " \- ,
at most 64 long.
.TP
.B list
List the files on the inbox, with their sizes.
.TP
.BI remove " NAME" ...
Take a program, or an app with everything in it, off the inbox. What the
firmware itself ships cannot be removed.
.TP
.BI push " NUMBER" ...
Put numbers on the calculator's stack, the last on top, as if they had been
typed and entered. A negative number needs no quoting:
.BR "qdosctl push 1 \-3 2" .
.TP
.BI run " LINE"
Evaluate a line, as if it had been typed on the input line and
.B ENTER
pressed: a word, an app, or a whole expression. Quote it if it has spaces.
.TP
.BI type " TEXT"
Type text as it is, character by character, without pressing
.BR ENTER .
.TP
.BI key " NAME" ...
Press keys, in order:
.BR enter ,
.BR esc ,
.BR del ,
.BR tab ,
.BR up ,
.BR down ,
.BR left ,
.BR right ,
.BR f1 " to " f5
(the soft keys), and
.B power
(power off, saving the session).
.TP
.B console
An interactive terminal on the calculator: what is typed goes to it as keys,
and its log is shown.
.B Ctrl\-]
leaves.
.TP
.B log
Print the calculator's log as it comes, until interrupted. With stack logging
built in, every change to the stack is a line of it.
.TP
.B ping
Check that QDOS is running and answering.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " \fIDEVICE\fR"
The serial port. Without it, the calculator is found by its USB name under
.IR /dev/serial/by\-id ,
and failing that the first
.I /dev/ttyACM*
is taken.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print only errors.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the commands and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QDOS_PORT
The serial port, when
.B \-\-port
is not given.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, nothing is coloured.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything was done.
.TP
.B 1
The calculator refused something: a file that did not arrive whole, or a name
that is not on the inbox.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong.
.TP
.B 3
The calculator could not be reached: no port, no permission, the port in use,
or QDOS not answering.
.SH EXAMPLES
Put a program and an app on the calculator, and see that they are there:
.PP
.RS
.nf
qdosctl upload hello.qd ../qdos\-doom/doom
qdosctl list
.fi
.RE
.PP
Solve x\(ha2 \- 3x + 2 = 0 with the shipped
.B quad
app, which takes its coefficients off the stack:
.PP
.RS
.nf
qdosctl push 1 \-3 2
qdosctl run quad
.fi
.RE
.PP
Open the menu of apps and start the first one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
qdosctl key f2 enter
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/serial/by\-id/usb\-Espressif*
The calculator, as udev names it.
.SH NOTES
Using the port needs membership of the group that owns it:
.B uucp
on Arch Linux,
.B dialout
on Debian and Ubuntu.
.B qdosctl
names the group when it is refused.
.PP
The protocol is a Ctrl\-B, then one line \(em
.BI PUT " size crc32 path" ,
.BI DEL " path" ,
.B LS
or
.B HELLO
\(em then, for
.BR PUT ,
the file's bytes. The calculator answers each with a line beginning
.BR "QDUP " ,
among its log. See
.I esp32/main/upload.c
in the QDOS sources.
.PP
Native modules cannot be uploaded: the calculator has no dynamic linker, so
they have to be built into its firmware.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stty (1),
.BR picocom (1)
